          <h2 id="french" data-faq-section>Writing French</h2>

          <details class="notice" data-faq open>
            <summary>How do I type accented capitals: É, È, À, Ç?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>Two ways to do it:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>turn on <kbd>Caps&nbsp;Lock</kbd>, then type the accented letter: <span class="insecable"><kbd>é</kbd> → É</span>, <span class="insecable"><kbd>è</kbd> → È</span>, <span class="insecable"><kbd>à</kbd> → À</span>, <span class="insecable"><kbd>ç</kbd> → Ç</span>;</li>
                <li>or type <kbd>^</kbd> then the accented letter.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                In AZERTY Global, Caps Lock puts accents in capitals by itself;
                punctuation and digits are not affected. On the traditional Windows AZERTY, É and Ç
                need an Alt code or a copy and paste; È and À go through
                <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>7</kbd></span> then <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + the vowel</span>. None can be typed in one keystroke.
              </p>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2 liens-rangee">
                Detailed guides: <a href="/e-aigu-majuscule" hreflang="fr">Capital É<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> ·
                <a href="/e-grave-majuscule" hreflang="fr">Capital È<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> ·
                <a href="/c-cedille-majuscule" hreflang="fr">Capital Ç<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> ·
                <a href="/a-grave-majuscule" hreflang="fr">Capital À<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq id="french-punctuation">
            <summary>How do I type French punctuation: «&nbsp;», the em dash, nonbreaking spaces…?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                Careful French typography uses the quotation marks <strong>« »</strong>,
                nonbreaking spaces, accented capitals, the apostrophe <strong>’</strong>, the
                dashes <strong>–</strong> and <strong>—</strong>, and the ligatures <strong>œ</strong>
                and <strong>æ</strong>. AZERTY Global gives you all of these:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>«&nbsp;»</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>W</kbd> / <kbd>X</kbd></span>;</li>
                <li><strong>—</strong> (em dash): <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>T</kbd></span>; <strong>–</strong> (en dash): <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>T</kbd></span>;</li>
                <li>narrow nonbreaking space: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd></span>; nonbreaking space: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd></span>;</li>
                <li><strong>’</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>4</kbd></span>;</li>
                <li><strong>œ</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>O</kbd></span>; <strong>æ</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>A</kbd></span>.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <a href="/en/french-typography">Writing correct French</a> details the rules,
                the francophone variants and professional uses, with the exact characters.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>Where did @, #, ù, %, ° and ² go?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <ul>
                <li><strong><a class="lien-caractere" href="/arobase" hreflang="fr">@<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></strong>: direct access on the former <kbd>²</kbd> key, at the top left, next to the 1.</li>
                <li><strong>#</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>@</kbd></span> on the same key, or <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>:</kbd></span>.</li>
                <li><strong>^</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>I</kbd></span>, as a live circumflex (typed directly, not a dead key).</li>
                <li><strong>ù</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>U</kbd></span>, or <span class="insecable"><kbd>´</kbd> then <kbd>V</kbd></span>. For <strong>Ù</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>U</kbd></span>.</li>
                <li><strong>%</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>)</kbd></span>, the key to the right of the 0.</li>
                <li><strong>°</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>)</kbd></span>, same key.</li>
                <li><strong>²</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>^</kbd> then <kbd>2</kbd></span>. All superscripts follow: <span class="insecable"><kbd>^</kbd> then a digit</span> gives ⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹.</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
                The ² key was reassigned to the at sign. The ù and the % moved to free the
                key to the right of the M, which now carries three dead-key accents: acute, grave and tilde.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <h2 id="languages" data-faq-section>Languages and symbols</h2>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>Can I write other European languages with AZERTY Global?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>Yes, without switching layouts. AZERTY&nbsp;Global lets you write <strong>more than 300&nbsp;languages</strong> in Latin, Greek or Cyrillic script, including:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Spanish</strong>: ñ, ¿, ¡</li>
                <li><strong>German</strong>: ä, ö, ü, ß</li>
                <li><strong>Portuguese</strong>: ã, õ, ç</li>
                <li><strong>Italian</strong>: à, è, ì, ò, ù</li>
                <li><strong>Nordic languages</strong>: þ, ø, å</li>
                <li>and Polish, Czech, Hungarian, Romanian, Turkish.</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
                The <a href="/en/guide">quick guide</a> shows the complete cheat sheet, layer
                by layer, and it prints.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq id="african-languages">
            <summary>How do I type the letters of African languages: Wolof, Bambara, Fula, Yoruba, Ewe?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                <strong>AZERTY Global natively gives more than 120 characters of African Latin-script
                languages, capitals included, that the traditional Windows AZERTY does not.</strong> No Keyman, no WinCompose,
                no copy and paste: everything goes through three dead keys, Extended Latin
                <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span>, Hook above
                <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>1</kbd>)</span> and Dot below
                <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>3</kbd>)</span>.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="notice__contenu">
              <div class="tableau-compare">
                <table role="table">
                  <caption>African characters and the exact keystrokes.</caption>
                  <thead role="rowgroup">
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="columnheader" scope="col">Character</th>
                      <th role="columnheader" scope="col">How to type it</th>
                      <th role="columnheader" scope="col">Languages</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody role="rowgroup">
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ɛ Ɛ</strong>: open e</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Extended Latin <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>E</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>E</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Bambara, Ewe, Fon, Dyula, Lingala</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ɔ Ɔ</strong>: open o</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Extended Latin <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>O</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>O</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Bambara, Ewe, Fon, Dyula, Lingala</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ŋ Ŋ</strong>: eng</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Extended Latin <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>N</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>N</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Wolof, Bambara, Fula, Hausa, Ewe</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ɲ Ɲ</strong>: palatal n</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Extended Latin <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>J</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>J</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Bambara, Fula, Wolof, Dyula</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ɓ Ɓ</strong>: implosive b</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Hook above <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>1</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>B</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>B</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Fula, Hausa</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ɗ Ɗ</strong>: implosive d</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Hook above <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>1</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>D</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>D</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Fula, Hausa</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ẹ Ẹ · ọ Ọ · ṣ Ṣ</strong></th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Dot below <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>3</kbd>)</span> then the letter</td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Yoruba, Igbo</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ƴ Ƴ</strong>: y with hook</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Extended Latin <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>Y</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Y</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Fula, Hausa</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ƙ Ƙ</strong>: k with hook</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Hook above <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>1</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>K</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>K</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Hausa</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr role="row">
                      <th role="rowheader" scope="row"><strong>ɖ Ɖ</strong>: d with tail</th>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="How to type it">Extended Latin <span class="insecable">(<kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>6</kbd>)</span> then <kbd>R</kbd> or <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd></span></td>
                      <td role="cell" data-colonne="Languages">Ewe, Fon</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                For tonal languages such as Bambara, Yoruba or Ewe, the acute, grave,
                circumflex, caron, tilde and macron accents can go on all these letters: type the
                letter, then the accent’s dead key twice <span class="insecable">(<kbd>´</kbd> <kbd>´</kbd></span> for the acute).
              </p>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
                Country by country: <a href="/afrique" hreflang="fr">what it changes in Africa<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>Where are the code symbols { } [ ] \ | ~ `?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                The symbols that structure code form the <strong>code belt</strong>:
                grouped on the home row, within reach without moving your hands with
                <kbd>AltGr</kbd>.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong><a class="lien-caractere" href="/accolades" hreflang="fr">{&nbsp;}<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>D</kbd> and <kbd>F</kbd></span></li>
                <li><strong><a class="lien-caractere" href="/crochets" hreflang="fr">[&nbsp;]<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>J</kbd> and <kbd>K</kbd></span></li>
                <li><strong>\</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>G</kbd></span></li>
                <li><strong>|</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>H</kbd></span></li>
              </ul>
              <p>The others follow, close by:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>#</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>@</kbd></span>, or <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>:</kbd></span></li>
                <li><strong>^</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>I</kbd></span>, live circumflex</li>
                <li><strong>`</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>L</kbd></span>, live grave accent</li>
                <li><strong>~</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>N</kbd></span>, live tilde</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
                <a href="/dev" hreflang="fr">All the symbols for developers<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>How do I type common symbols such as →, ×, ©, •, ½, ≠?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>Through dead keys, which open hundreds of characters.</p>

              <h3>With AltGr</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>≤</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>&lt;</kbd></span></li>
                <li><strong>≥</strong>: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>&lt;</kbd></span></li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Scientific symbols dead key: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>=</kbd></span></h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>±</strong>: then <kbd>Space</kbd></li>
                <li><strong>≠</strong>: then <kbd>=</kbd></li>
                <li><strong>×</strong>: then <kbd>X</kbd></li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Miscellaneous symbols dead key: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>@</kbd></span></h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>→</strong>: then <kbd>D</kbd>, or <kbd>Space</kbd></li>
                <li><strong>©</strong>: then <kbd>C</kbd></li>
                <li><strong>½</strong>: then <kbd>2</kbd></li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Punctuation symbols dead key: <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>!</kbd></span></h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>•</strong> the bullet: then <kbd>:</kbd></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </details>

          <h2 id="discover" data-faq-section>Discovering AZERTY Global</h2>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>What is AZERTY Global?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                A French keyboard layout that fixes the flaws of the traditional Windows AZERTY
                without replacing it: the letters A to Z, é, è, à and ç stay in place, and only the ù is moved.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>accented capitals É, È, À, Ç in one keystroke, with <kbd>Caps&nbsp;Lock</kbd> on;</li>
                <li>French quotation marks « » with no detour;</li>
                <li>œ, æ and the missing characters built in.</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2 liens-rangee">
                <a href="/en/guide">The five changes in two minutes</a> ·
                <a href="/en/comparison">The comparison, with figures</a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>How long does it take to adjust?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                AZERTY Global changes only <strong>five things</strong>. They are quick to memorize; the
                reflexes finish settling in after a few days of use.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>99% of keystrokes are unchanged</strong>: the share of keystrokes affected
                is 1.01%, measured on the frequencies of the AFNOR corpus (half formal, half
                informal text, <a href="/en/comparison#methode-comparatif">method</a>). The letters and
                digits stay in place.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq id="stickers">
            <summary>Do I need to change keyboards or buy stickers?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                <strong>No.</strong> AZERTY Global is purely software: your current keyboard
                works. There is, incidentally, no physical keyboard engraved with AZERTY Global yet.
              </p>
              <p>
                The letters and digits stay in place. For the few new characters, muscle
                memory settles in quickly; stickers can help during the first days,
                but are not necessary.
              </p>
              <h3>What exists today</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>To print at home:</strong> PDFs are included in the download, to print on sticker paper.</li>
                <li><strong>Ready-to-apply stickers:</strong> no set is currently on sale. If a ready-to-apply set or an engraved keyboard interests you, <a href="/autocollants-claviers?depuis=/en/faq#formulaire" hreflang="fr">ask to be notified<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>AZERTY Global or AFNOR: which one should I choose?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>Both fix the Windows AZERTY, with two opposite logics.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong>AZERTY Global</strong>: five improvements, smooth transition, a Caps Lock
                  that accents, code symbols on the home row. Free, open source,
                  for Windows, macOS and Linux.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>AFNOR, the NF Z71-300 standard</strong>: shipped with Windows 11 since 24H2
                  as “French (Standard, AZERTY)”. It repositions 33 characters that are already
                  present, 31 of which change key, across 22 keys. Accented capitals there
                  are typed with <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd></span>.
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                The count does not tell the whole story: twelve moves that follow a rule cost
                less than isolated positions to learn one by one. AZERTY Global values
                continuity with what you already know; the AFNOR layout reorganizes the signs other
                than letters and digits more systematically, at the price of broader relearning.
              </p>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
                <a href="/en/comparison">The comparison, row by row and with figures</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq id="tester-privacy">
            <summary>Does the online tester record what I type?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                <strong>No.</strong> The tester translates your keystrokes within the page: nothing you
                type is sent or recorded, and nothing is written to your device. Closing
                the tab erases everything.
              </p>
              <p>
                Audience measurement only counts the steps of the course reached, never the text.
                <a href="/confidentialite#testeur" hreflang="fr">The details are in the privacy policy<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <h2 id="install" data-faq-section>Installing and using</h2>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>Is it compatible with video games, with ZQSD?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                <strong>For ZQSD movement, yes.</strong> The letters A to Z stay in place:
                the physical keys stay where they are, and so do your controls.
              </p>
              <p>
                Some games, however, depend on the console key, a shortcut or an
                anti-cheat system. <a href="/en/compatibility">The compatibility page</a> lists the known cases
                and their solutions.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>How do I keep AZERTY Global as the default after a restart?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <h3>Microsoft Store app</h3>
              <p>
                Right-click the AG icon in the taskbar, then
                <strong>Launch at Windows startup</strong>.
              </p>

              <h3>EXE installer</h3>
              <p>If Windows goes back to your old keyboard after a restart:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>The simplest way:</strong> remove the keyboards you no longer need, in <em>Settings → Time &amp; language → Language &amp; region → French (…) → Language options → Keyboards</em>.</li>
                <li><strong>If you need to keep several:</strong> <em>Settings → Time &amp; language → Typing → Advanced keyboard settings → “Override for default input method”</em>, and choose <strong>AZERTY Global</strong>.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>macOS</h3>
              <p>
                Once added in <em>System Settings → Keyboard → Text Input</em> (before
                macOS 13: <em>System Preferences → Keyboard → Input Sources</em>), it stays
                available after a restart. Remove the other keyboards so that it is the only one active.
              </p>

              <h3>Linux</h3>
              <p>The installation script sets it up permanently; it survives a restart.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>Is it available on Mac and Linux?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>
                Yes: <strong>Windows</strong>, <strong>macOS</strong> 10.12 or later, and
                <strong>Linux</strong> (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, Arch).
              </p>
              <p>
                On Windows, the Microsoft Store app targets Windows 10 version 1809 or later
                and Windows 11. The EXE installer remains the channel for computers without the Store.
              </p>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
                <a href="/en/download">Download for your platform</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq id="uninstall">
            <summary>How do I uninstall AZERTY Global?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>Without risk: you can reinstall AZERTY Global at any time.</p>

              <h3>Microsoft Store app</h3>
              <p>
                <span class="insecable"><kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>I</kbd></span> → <strong>Apps</strong> →
                <strong>Installed apps</strong> → search for “AZERTY Global” →
                <strong>Uninstall</strong> (Windows 10: <strong>Apps</strong> →
                <strong>Apps &amp; features</strong>).
              </p>

              <h3>EXE installer</h3>
              <ol>
                <li>Remove the keyboard: <em>Settings → Time &amp; language → Language &amp; region → French (…) → Language options → AZERTY Global → Remove</em>.</li>
                <li>Run the EXE installer again to finish the uninstallation.</li>
              </ol>

              <h3>macOS</h3>
              <ol>
                <li>Remove the keyboard in <em>System Settings → Keyboard → Text Input</em> (before macOS 13: <em>System Preferences → Keyboard → Input Sources</em>).</li>
                <li>Delete <code>AZERTY Global.keylayout</code> from <code>~/Library/Keyboard Layouts/</code>, or from <code>/Library/Keyboard Layouts/</code> if you installed it for all users.</li>
                <li>Restart your session.</li>
              </ol>

              <h3>Linux</h3>
              <ol>
                <li>Remove AZERTY Global from your input sources.</li>
                <li>Delete the file: <code>sudo rm /usr/share/X11/xkb/symbols/azerty_global</code></li>
                <li>Restore your previous <code>~/.XCompose</code> if needed: the installation saved it as <code>~/.XCompose.backup.</code> followed by the date and time.</li>
                <li>Restart your session.</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="notice" data-faq>
            <summary>Can it be installed in a company?</summary>
            <div class="notice__contenu prose">
              <p>Yes. Which channel to use depends on your IT rules: the channel depends on your IT rules.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Microsoft Store allowed:</strong> the app installs without administrator rights and without a SmartScreen warning, with a virtual keyboard, character search and lessons.</li>
                <li><strong>Microsoft Store blocked:</strong> the AMCF-signed MSIX installs directly or is deployed outside the Store by the IT department.</li>
                <li><strong>EXE installer:</strong> classic installation in Windows, for computers without the Store; administrator rights required, SmartScreen possible.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                With the app (Microsoft Store or MSIX),
                <span class="insecable"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Caps&nbsp;Lock</kbd></span> suspends and
                re-enables AZERTY Global instantly, with no effect on your shortcuts; with
                the EXE installer, you go back to your usual AZERTY in the language settings.
              </p>
              <p class="texte-petit texte-2 liens-rangee">
                <a href="/entreprises" hreflang="fr">For companies<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> · <a href="/ecoles" hreflang="fr">For schools<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

        </div>

